  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 123 PM CDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nConfidence is high in the overall pattern: cool, dry, and sunny this\nweekend, then progressively warmer and still mostly dry through \nmidweek, with only low-confidence, low-probability showers late in\nthe week. The main forecast uncertainties lie in the exact magnitude\nof radiational cooling Sunday morning (and thus frost coverage in \nborderline areas), and the timing and extent of any late-week \nprecipitation as the next trough approaches.\n\nThe widespread freeze last night will effectively end the\ngrowing season for sensitive vegetation across much of the area. The\ncold surface high is forecast to shift off to the east today and \nallow for southerly warm-air advection to begin. Enough warmer \nsoutherly flow is favored tonight to keep temperatures above freezing\nacross the area. Some of the river valleys may see some temperatures\nin the mid 30s and potentially some patchy frost.\n\nThe bulk of the warming trend occurs Sunday through midweek as\nstronger mid-level ridging builds over the Plains. Highs are \nforecast to reach the 60s Sunday and Monday, then the 70s Tuesday, \nand Wednesday and potentially the 80s Thursday and Friday, with \novernight lows moderating into the upper 40s to upper 50s. These \nvalues represent a return to above- normal temperatures by midweek, \ngiven climatological normals in the 60s/40s for mid-October in \nnortheast Kansas.\n\nPrecipitation chances remain very low through at least Wednesday.\nA deeper western trough approaches late next week, but with timing and\ndetails are still uncertain.",
